.TH PKCSICSF 1 "September 2026" "@PACKAGE_VERSION@" "openCryptoki"
.SH NAME
pkcsicsf \- configuration utility for the ICSF token

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpkcsicsf\fP
[\fB-h\fP]
[\fB-l\fP]
[\fB-a\fP \fItoken-name\fP]
\fB-m\fP \fImechanism\fP
[\fB-u\fP \fIURI\fP]
[\fB-b\fP \fIBINDDN\fP]
[\fB-c\fP \fIclient-cert-file\fP]
[\fB-C\fP \fICA-cert-file\fP]
[\fB-k\fP \fIprivatekey\fP]
.br
\fBpkcsicsf\fP \fB-p\fP \fItoken-name\fP

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBpkcsicsf\fP utility lists available ICSF tokens, adds a specific
ICSF token to opencryptoki, and updates the locally stored RACF password
for an existing token.

The ICSF token must be added first to opencryptoki. This creates an
entry in the opencryptoki.conf file for the ICSF token. It also creates
a \fItoken_name.conf\fP configuration file in the same directory as
the opencryptoki.conf file, containing ICSF specific information.
This information is read by the ICSF token.

The ICSF token must bind and authenticate to an LDAP server.
The supported authentication mechanisms are simple and sasl.
One of these mechanisms must be entered when listing the available
ICSF tokens or when adding an ICSF token.

The system admin can either allow the ldap calls to utilize existing
ldap configs, such as ldap.conf or .ldaprc for bind and
authentication information or set the bind and authentication
information within opencryptoki by using this utility and its options.
The information will then be placed in the \fItoken_name.conf\fP file
to be used in the ldap calls. When using simple authentication,
the user will be prompted for the racf password when listing
or adding a token.

The \fBpkcsicsf\fP utility must be run as root when adding an ICSF token to
opencryptoki.

.SH "TOKEN INITIALIZATION"
Unlike other openCryptoki tokens, the ICSF token does \fBnot\fP require
\fBC_InitToken\fP (i.e.
.BR pkcsconf (1)
\fB-I\fP) to be run after adding the token.
The cryptographic objects and key material reside on the remote z/OS ICSF
server, which is already fully provisioned before \fBpkcsicsf\fP is invoked.
Running \fBC_InitToken\fP on an ICSF token is therefore unnecessary.

When \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP succeeds it performs the full local initialization
automatically:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
Creates the token directory under \fI@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fP.
.IP \(bu 4
Creates the ICSF-specific configuration file
\fI@sysconfdir@/opencryptoki/token_name.conf\fP.
.IP \(bu 4
Writes the initial token data file (\fINVTOK.DAT\fP) with:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
The SO PIN supplied during \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBCKF_TOKEN_INITIALIZED\fP already set \(em no further init step is needed.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBCKF_USER_PIN_TO_BE_CHANGED\fP set, indicating the user PIN must be
established before first use.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBtokversion = 3.28\fP recorded in \fIopencryptoki.conf\fP, enabling the
FIPS-compliant data store format (see
.B "TOKEN DATA FORMAT"
below).
.RE
.IP \(bu 4
Writes the RACF file (\fIRACF\fP) with the RACF password encrypted using
AES-256-GCM under the token master key.
.IP \(bu 4
Writes \fIMK_SO\fP with the token master key wrapped using AES-256 Key Wrap
(RFC\~3394) under a PBKDF2-SHA-512 derived key.
.RE

The correct post-installation sequence is therefore:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fP
Add the token (run as root):
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsicsf \-a \fItoken-name\fB \-m simple \-u ldap://\fIhost\fR\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB2.\fP
Restart the slot daemon so the new token is visible:
.PP
.RS
\fBsystemctl restart pkcsslotd\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB3.\fP
Initialize the user PIN (run as SO):
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsconf \-u \-c \fIslot\fR\fP
.RE
.RE
.PP
After step\~3 the token is fully operational and
\fBCKF_TOKEN_INITIALIZED\fP will be reported by
.BR pkcsconf (1)
\fB\-t\fP.

.SH "RACF PASSWORD CHANGE"
When the RACF password for a bind DN is changed on the z/OS ICSF server,
the locally stored encrypted copy must be updated using \fBpkcsicsf \-p\fP.
No token removal or re-initialization is required; the cryptographic
objects on the z/OS server are not affected.
.PP
Procedure:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fP
Change the RACF password on the z/OS server (outside of openCryptoki).
.TP
\fB2.\fP
Run \fBpkcsicsf \-p\fP \fBimmediately\fP afterwards:
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsicsf \-p \fItoken-name\fR\fP
.RE
.PP
The authentication mechanism, URI, and bind DN are read automatically
from the existing token configuration.
You will be prompted for the new RACF password and then for the SO PIN.
The new RACF password is first verified against the LDAP server.
If that succeeds, the master key is unwrapped using the SO PIN and the
RACF file is re-encrypted with the new password.
.RE
.PP
No restart of \fBpkcsslotd\fP or of any application using the token is
required.
Both the v3 token format (tokversion\~=\~3.28) and the legacy format are
supported.
.PP
.B "Operational timing requirement \(em minimise the update window"
.PP
There is an unavoidable service window between step\~1 and step\~2.
During this window, any new LDAP connection that the token needs to
establish \(em for a new \fBC_Login\fP or \fBC_OpenSession\fP call \(em
will attempt to bind with the old, now-invalid RACF password and will
fail.
The caller will receive \fBCKR_FUNCTION_FAILED\fP or
\fBCKR_USER_NOT_LOGGED_IN\fP until \fBpkcsicsf \-p\fP has been run.
.PP
\fBAlready-established LDAP connections are not affected:\fP a session
that completed \fBC_Login\fP before the password was changed on the z/OS
server retains a live, authenticated LDAP connection and continues to
operate normally throughout the window.
.PP
To keep the window as short as possible, run \fBpkcsicsf \-p\fP
immediately after the RACF password is changed on z/OS.
Repeated failed bind attempts during the window may trigger the RACF
account lockout policy on the z/OS server; consult your z/OS security
administrator for the applicable lockout threshold.

.SH "TOKEN DATA FORMAT"
Tokens added by \fBpkcsicsf\fP always use the FIPS-compliant data store
format introduced in openCryptoki 3.28.
This is indicated by \fBtokversion = 3.28\fP in the slot entry in
\fIopencryptoki.conf\fP.
.PP
The format uses the following security properties:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
\fINVTOK.DAT\fP \(em PIN verification and master-key wrapping use
PBKDF2-SHA-512 with 100,000 iterations and a 64-byte random salt.
The derived login key is stored for PIN verification; a separately
derived wrap key protects the master key.
.IP \(bu 4
\fIMK_SO\fP / \fIMK_USER\fP \(em the 256-bit AES token master key is
wrapped using AES-256 Key Wrap (RFC\~3394) under the PBKDF2-derived wrap
key.
The file contains exactly 40 bytes (the RFC\~3394 output) with no
additional header.
.IP \(bu 4
\fIRACF\fP \(em the RACF password is encrypted using AES-256-GCM.
The token name is used as Additional Authenticated Data (AAD), binding
the ciphertext to the specific token and detecting any tampering or
accidental file substitution.
The file contains a big-endian version field, a 12-byte random nonce,
a 16-byte GCM authentication tag, a big-endian ciphertext length, and
the ciphertext.
.RE
.PP
Existing ICSF tokens that do not have \fBtokversion = 3.28\fP in their
slot entry continue to use the previous data format and are not affected.

.SH "MIGRATION TO THE NEW DATA FORMAT"
There is no automated migration tool.
To migrate an existing ICSF token to the FIPS-compliant data format,
remove the token and add it again using \fBpkcsicsf\fP:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fP
Remove the existing slot entry from \fIopencryptoki.conf\fP and delete
the token's local data directory:
.PP
.RS
\fBrm \-rf @localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fR\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB2.\fP
Re-add the token (run as root):
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsicsf \-a \fItoken-name\fB \-m \fImechanism\fR \fI[options]\fR\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB3.\fP
Restart the slot daemon:
.PP
.RS
\fBsystemctl restart pkcsslotd\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB4.\fP
Re-initialize the user PIN (run as SO):
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsconf \-u \-c \fIslot\fR\fP
.RE
.RE
.PP
The cryptographic objects and key material reside on the remote z/OS ICSF
server and are not stored locally.
They are therefore \fBnot affected\fP by the migration and remain fully
available after the token is re-added.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.IP "\fB-a\fP \fItoken name\fP" 10
Add the specified ICSF token to opencryptoki.
See
.B TOKEN INITIALIZATION
above for the steps that follow. To add all available ICSF token,
specify \fBall\fP.
.IP "\fB-b\fP \fIBINDDN\fP" 10
The distinguished name to bind when using simple authentication.
.IP "\fB-c\fP \fIclient-cert-file\fP" 10
The client certificate file for SASL authentication.
If not specified, the value from \fBldap.conf\fP(5) or \fI~/.ldaprc\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-C\fP \fICA-cert-file\fP" 10
The CA certificate file for SASL authentication.
If not specified, the value from \fBldap.conf\fP(5) or \fI~/.ldaprc\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-h\fP" 10
Show usage information.
.IP "\fB-k\fP \fIprivatekey\fP" 10
The client private key file for SASL authentication.
If not specified, the value from \fBldap.conf\fP(5) or \fI~/.ldaprc\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-l\fP" 10
List available ICSF tokens.
.IP "\fB-m\fP \fImechanism\fP" 10
The authentication mechanism to use when binding to the LDAP server
(either \fBsimple\fP or \fBsasl\fP).
This option is required whenever \fB\-l\fP or \fB\-a\fP is used.
Must not be specified with \fB\-p\fP.
.IP "\fB-p\fP \fItoken-name\fP" 10
Update the locally stored RACF password for the named token after the
RACF password has been changed on the z/OS ICSF server.
The authentication mechanism, URI, and bind DN are read automatically from
the existing token configuration; \fB\-m\fP, \fB\-u\fP, and \fB\-b\fP
must not be specified.
Only tokens using simple authentication have a locally stored RACF password;
the command will refuse with an error if the token uses SASL.
The new RACF password is prompted for and verified by binding to the LDAP
server before anything is written locally.
The SO PIN is then prompted for to unwrap the token master key, which is
used to re-encrypt the RACF password in place.
The caller must be a member of the \fBpkcs11\fP group.
See
.B "RACF PASSWORD CHANGE"
below for the full procedure.
.IP "\fB-u\fP \fIURI\fP" 10
The URI of the LDAP server (e.g. \fBldap://hostname\fP or
\fBldaps://hostname\fP).

.SH "FILES"
.IP "@sysconfdir@/opencryptoki/opencryptoki.conf"
The opencryptoki config file containing token configuration information.
A slot entry for the ICSF token is added here by \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP,
including \fBtokversion = 3.28\fP to enable the FIPS-compliant data
store format.
.IP "@sysconfdir@/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP.conf"
Contains ICSF configuration information for the ICSF token.
.IP "@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP/NVTOK.DAT"
Local token data file.
Stores PBKDF2-SHA-512 PIN verification keys, wrap-key parameters, and
token flags including \fBCKF_TOKEN_INITIALIZED\fP.
Written by \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP and updated by \fBpkcsconf\fP.
.IP "@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP/MK_SO"
Contains the token master key wrapped under the SO PBKDF2-derived wrap
key using AES-256 Key Wrap (RFC\~3394).
Written by \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP and updated when the SO PIN is changed.
.IP "@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP/MK_USER"
Contains the token master key wrapped under the user PBKDF2-derived wrap
key using AES-256 Key Wrap (RFC\~3394).
Written when the user PIN is initialized or changed.
.IP "@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP/RACF"
Contains the RACF password encrypted with AES-256-GCM under the token
master key.
Written by \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP and updated by \fBpkcsicsf \-p\fP.

.SH SEE ALSO
.PD 0
.TP
\fBopencryptoki\fP(7),
.TP
\fBpkcsslotd\fP(8),
.TP
\fBpkcsconf\fP(1).
.PD
